The snippet below walks the same path the JVM does — a `put` that hashes to a bucket, an overwrite when the key `equals()` an existing one, and a resize once the table fills past its threshold.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class HashMapInternals {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();  // cap 16, lf 0.75
        map.put("apple", 1);   // hash("apple") -> index -> bucket
        map.put("apple", 2);   // same key (equals) -> overwrite to 2
        map.put("grape", 9);   // different bucket (or collision -> chain)

        // get uses the stored mixed hash to select a bin, then equals() pinpoints the key:
        int v = map.get("apple");  // 2

        // After ~12 entries (16 * 0.75) the table resizes to 32.
        for (int i = 0; i < 20; i++) map.put("k" + i, i);
    }
}
```
Nothing here is special syntax — it is plain Java 17. The machinery (hashing, chaining, resizing) all runs inside `put` and `get`, invisible to the caller.

Sharing a plain `HashMap` across threads corrupts it, and reaching for `Hashtable` to "fix" that just trades correctness for a whole-map bottleneck.

```java
import java.util.*;

// HashMap shared by threads -> data races, lost updates, infinite loops
Map<String, Integer> counts = new HashMap<>();
// thread A: counts.put(...);  thread B: counts.put(...);  // CORRUPTION

// Hashtable "works" but locks the WHOLE map on every call -> bottleneck
Map<String, Integer> legacy = new Hashtable<>();
```

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

// reads generally avoid locking, fine-grained write locking -> scales across threads
ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> counts = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
counts.merge("sku-1", 1, Integer::sum);   // atomic increment
counts.computeIfAbsent("sku-2", k -> 0);

// Single-threaded? Plain HashMap is fastest:
// Map<String,Integer> m = new HashMap<>();
```
For multi-threaded maps, reach for `ConcurrentHashMap` and its atomic helpers; never resurrect `Hashtable`.

Watch how HashMap accepts a null key and null values, while ConcurrentHashMap rejects them outright.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class NullKeysDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, String> map = new HashMap<>();
        map.put(null, "v");        // OK: one null key
        map.put(null, "w");        // overwrites -> value now "w"
        map.put("a", null);        // OK: null value
        map.put("b", null);        // OK: many null values

        // Distinguish missing key from null value:
        boolean present = map.containsKey("a");  // true
        String v = map.get("missing");           // null -> but key absent

        // ConcurrentHashMap forbids null entirely:
        Map<String, String> chm = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
        // chm.put(null, "x");     // NullPointerException
    }
}
```
The `containsKey("a")` check is the reliable way to ask 'is this key present?' — `get()` alone cannot tell you.

When you know how many entries are coming, pre-sizing skips the repeated resize-and-redistribution storms a default map would suffer.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class CapacityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Default: capacity 16, load factor 0.75 -> resizes at 12 entries
        Map<String, Integer> def = new HashMap<>();

        // Expecting 1000 entries? Pre-size to avoid repeated resizing:
        int expected = 1000;
        int cap = (int) (expected / 0.75f) + 1;     // ~1334
        Map<String, Integer> sized = new HashMap<>(cap);
        for (int i = 0; i < expected; i++) sized.put("k" + i, i);
        // No resize storms during the loop.

        // Custom load factor (rarely needed): more memory, fewer collisions
        Map<String, Integer> tight = new HashMap<>(64, 0.5f);
    }
}
```
Note `n / 0.75 + 1`, not `n` — capacity equal to `n` would still trip the 0.75 threshold and resize.

Each `put` and `get` hashes the key straight to a bucket — no scan — which is why both are constant time on average.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class HashMapComplexity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        // put: hash the key -> index -> place in bucket. O(1) average.
        map.put("sku-1", 100);
        map.put("sku-2", 200);

        // get: hash the key -> index -> read the bucket. O(1) average.
        Integer price = map.get("sku-1");   // 100

        // Worst case (all collide) is O(n), but since Java 8 a bucket
        // with > 8 entries becomes a red-black tree -> O(log n).
        // Note: iterating the whole map is always O(n + capacity).
        for (var e : map.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println(e.getKey() + "=" + e.getValue());
        }
    }
}
```
The `entrySet()` loop is the exception — iterating the whole map is O(n + capacity), since it touches every bucket.

With `equals()` overridden but no `hashCode()`, two logically equal `Point` keys get different identity hashes, land in different buckets, and the lookup misses.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Point {
    final int x, y;
    Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
        return o instanceof Point p && p.x == x && p.y == y;
    }
    // NO hashCode() override -> uses identity hash
}

Map<Point, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put(new Point(1, 2), "A");
String v = map.get(new Point(1, 2));  // null! different bucket
```

```java
import java.util.*;

class Point {
    final int x, y;
    Point(int x, int y) { this.x = x; this.y = y; }
    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
        return o instanceof Point p && p.x == x && p.y == y;
    }
    @Override public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(x, y);   // SAME fields as equals
    }
}

Map<Point, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put(new Point(1, 2), "A");
String v = map.get(new Point(1, 2));  // "A" - found
```
Use `Objects.hash(...)` / `Objects.equals(...)`, or a `record`, which generates both correctly from the same fields.
